            <div xml:id="d280">

                <pb facs="0250" n="240" sameAs="frus:frus1941v06/pg240"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">722.2315/2555: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Norweb</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-11-18T13:00:00-05:00">November 18, 1941—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>618. For the Under Secretary. Your telegram 461, November 17, 9 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">8</hi>] p.m. My Argentine and Brazilian colleagues think
                    the move is a desirable one but they wish to have the Argentine and Brazilian
                    Ambassadors at Washington clearly understand that the Peruvian view is that the
                    1936 <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> line can serve as a basis but with
                    substantial modifications in favor of Peru in the Oriente (Andoas and
                    Rocafuerte).</p>
                <pb facs="0251" n="241" xml:id="pg241"/>
                <p>We are agreed that for political reasons no mention should be made of President
                    Prado, but his statements could be used as representing the views of the
                    Peruvian Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Norweb</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
